# Line endings and encoding, decided here rather than by whoever clones this.
#
# **There was no .gitattributes at all**, which means every file's line endings
# were whatever each machine's `core.autocrlf` happened to be. On one developer
# that is invisible. On two it is a diff of the entire repository the first time
# somebody saves a file, and this project has one rule that is *encoding*
# sensitive and has already been broken once, silently, with no log.

# The default: normalise to LF in the repository, and let Git give each
# working tree what its platform expects. Text detection is automatic.
* text=auto

# --- PowerShell: ASCII with a BOM, and this is a non-negotiable -------------
#
# `install.ps1` and `add-to-path.ps1` are run by Windows PowerShell 5.1, which
# assumes the system ANSI code page for a file with no BOM. A BOM-less file
# containing a non-ASCII character - an em dash pasted from a document, a
# curly quote from an editor - is decoded as mojibake and fails at parse time
# with no error anybody can act on. It killed the installer once already; see
# `.vscode/settings.json`, which forces `utf8bom` for exactly this reason.
#
# **`-text`: never touched, never re-encoded, byte for byte.** The first
# version of this line said `working-tree-encoding=UTF-8BOM`, which is the
# textbook answer and is wrong here. That directive expects the *stored* blob
# to be BOM-less UTF-8, with Git adding the BOM on checkout - but these files
# were committed with their BOMs years ago, so Git could not round-trip them
# and every `git status` printed `failed to encode 'install.ps1' from UTF-8BOM
# to UTF-8`. Fixing it properly would mean renormalising the stored bytes of
# the four scripts whose encoding is the thing being protected, which is a
# risk taken to satisfy a mechanism rather than a need.
#
# The files are already correct on disk and already correct in the repository.
# What is wanted is for Git to stop having opinions about them, and `-text` is
# exactly that: no line-ending conversion, no encoding conversion, the bytes
# that were committed are the bytes that are checked out. A clone on any
# machine, with any `core.autocrlf`, gets a working installer.
*.ps1 -text
*.psm1 -text

# --- Windows batch: CRLF, because cmd.exe is unforgiving --------------------
# A `.cmd` with LF endings can execute its last line without a terminator or
# mis-parse a label, which is a class of failure that looks like the script
# being wrong rather than the file being wrong.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# --- Everything the tools care about, kept as LF ---------------------------
*.py text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf

# --- Binary: never normalised, never diffed --------------------------------
# Git guessing at these mangles them. `.pst` and the Office formats are here
# because fixtures of those types have existed in this tree before.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.pst binary
*.db binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.msg binary
*.onnx binary

# --- Visual Studio project files -------------------------------------------
# Generated by `scripts/regen_vs_project.py`, so a merge conflict in one is
# noise: regenerate rather than resolve. CRLF because Visual Studio rewrites
# them that way on every save, and a whole-file diff each time it opens the
# solution is worse than the setting.
*.pyproj text eol=crlf
*.sln text eol=crlf
